<poml>
    <task>
        Generate a single-log health report card: inventory what topics actually exist in
        the log, run only the checks those topics support, and back every number in the
        report with a Bagel SQL query.
        <stepwise-instructions>
            <list listStyle="decimal">
                <item>
                    Call `describe_data_source` on the path. Note the log identity (path,
                    format), start/end time, duration, message count, and the full topic
                    list - this determines which checks below are even possible. Do not
                    guess topic names; read them from this call.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Match the topics that exist to the check categories below by name first -
                    `describe_data_source` returns topic names only, not types. Topic names
                    differ per ecosystem; treat these as hints, not requirements, and adapt to
                    whatever the log actually contains:
                    <list>
                        <item>
                            <b>PX4 (.ulg)</b>: `battery_status_*` (power),
                            `sensor_accel_*`/`sensor_combined_*` (IMU),
                            `vehicle_gps_position_*` (GPS),
                            `vehicle_status_*`/`commander_state_*` (status).
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>ArduPilot (.bin/.log)</b>: `BAT` (power), `IMU`/`VIBE` (IMU),
                            `GPS` (GPS), `MSG` (status text).
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>Betaflight (.bbl/.BFL)</b>: the `I`/`P` frame topics carry
                            `vbatLatest` (power) and `gyroADC[0..2]`/`gyroUnfilt[0..2]` (IMU)
                            in one table; treat GPS as absent unless a GPS frame type shows
                            up in `describe_data_source`.
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>ROS 1/2 (.bag/.mcap/.db3)</b>: a topic typed
                            `sensor_msgs/BatteryState` (power), `sensor_msgs/Imu` (IMU),
                            `sensor_msgs/NavSatFix` (GPS), and `/rosout` or a `Log`-typed
                            topic (status).
                        </item>
                    </list>
                    If a check's expected topics aren't found by name, don't rule it out yet:
                    call `describe_topic` on any plausibly-relevant or ambiguous topic to
                    check its fields before declaring the check skipped - type only becomes
                    available from `describe_topic`, in the next step (e.g. a ROS topic named
                    `/foo`, with no obvious semantics, may still carry `BatteryState` fields).
                    Only once no topic - by name or by checked fields - matches is a check
                    SKIPPED, not failed; never fabricate a reading for a topic that doesn't
                    exist.
                </item>
                <item>
                    For each applicable check, call `describe_topic` once to confirm the
                    exact field paths and units (they vary by firmware/message version),
                    then answer with `query_messages` SQL. Never estimate a number from
                    `describe_data_source` metadata alone - it does not contain message
                    values.
                    <list>
                        <item>
                            <b>Power</b>: min, max, and end-of-log voltage, plus the largest
                            single-sample drop (e.g.
                            `LAG(voltage) OVER (ORDER BY timestamp_seconds) - voltage`, so
                            MAX() surfaces the largest drop). Flag sustained sag toward the
                            pack's empty voltage, or a sudden drop out of proportion to the
                            rest of the log.
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>IMU / vibration</b>: variance and max absolute value of linear
                            acceleration (or gyro) per axis. Flag a window whose variance or
                            extreme is far outside the log's own baseline (e.g. several times
                            the overall STDDEV/AVG), and say when it happens.
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>GPS</b>: the fix-quality distribution (fix type / status /
                            satellite count, whatever the ecosystem exposes) and any stretch
                            where fix quality drops or the topic stops publishing. Flag a
                            low-fix majority or a dropout, with its duration.
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>Data gaps</b>: for the topics already queried above (plus any
                            other topic central to the log), compute inter-message latency
                            from `timestamp_seconds` - the `diagnose/latency` recipe. Flag a
                            gap much larger than the topic's own median interval.
                        </item>
                        <item>
                            <b>Errors / status</b>: call `read_loggings` for the log's time
                            range. If it returns nothing, or errors as unsupported for this
                            format (e.g. Betaflight, which has no logging-message support),
                            fall back to a status/text topic instead (ArduPilot `MSG`, ROS
                            `/rosout`) and query it with `query_messages`. Flag any ERROR;
                            flag WARN only if numerous. If neither `read_loggings` nor a
                            status/text topic is available, this check is SKIPPED - say why.
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </item>
                <item>
                    Every numeric claim in the report must trace to a query you actually ran.
                    Keep each query on hand and show it if the user asks "how do you know" -
                    do not print every query inline in the report itself.
                </item>
            </list>
        </stepwise-instructions>
    </task>

    <hint>
        Report card skeleton - one line per section, icon first, numbers not adjectives:
        <code lang="text">
            sample.ulg - 41.5s, 2018 messages, 74 topics

            Power      ✅  min 22.1V, largest drop 0.3V
            IMU        ⚠️  accel_z variance 4.2x the log baseline at t=28s
            GPS        —  skipped: no GPS topic
            Data gaps  ✅  no gap > 3x median interval (checked battery_status_0, sensor_accel_0)
            Errors     ❌  2 ERROR messages, first at t=31s: "failsafe triggered"

            VERDICT: investigate the t=31s failsafe error first.
        </code>
    </hint>

    <output-format>
        A compact report card, in this exact shape:
        <list listStyle="decimal">
            <item>
                One header line: log identity (path or file name) and duration, plus
                message count and topic count if useful for context.
            </item>
            <item>
                One line per check that was actually run: the check name, a
                ✅ (clear) / ⚠️ (worth a look) / ❌ (actionable problem) verdict, and the
                1-2 key numbers that justify it. No prose paragraphs per check.
            </item>
            <item>
                One line per check that was skipped, using a `—` marker and a short reason
                naming what was missing (e.g. "skipped: no GPS topic").
            </item>
            <item>
                A final `VERDICT:` line naming the single most actionable item across every
                check that ran (worst verdict first; ties broken by earliest timestamp), or
                stating all-clear if every check that ran passed.
            </item>
        </list>
        Do not include the raw SQL in the report card; offer to show it only if asked.
    </output-format>
</poml>
